  <sharedRules>
    <rule id="8e82-d31c-230f-a046" name="Thematic Byzantine (AoA2) - List Notes" publicationId="fcb9-17be-94c2-bcfc" hidden="false">
      <description>ARMIES OF ANTIQUITY v2 #61 (unofficial, Martin Gibbins). Probably under Heraclius around 660 AD, the Empire was divided into Themata (provinces), each of which provided its own armed forces through a system similar to feudalism, whereby soldiers gave military service in return for grants of land. Later, around 750 AD, this system was augmented by the introduction of the Tagmata, a full-time professional force based around Constantinople. This system effectively mirrored the late Roman one of frontier and mobile field forces. During this period the Empire came under extreme pressure from both the Arabs and the Bulgars. Despite all odds, it held on and was often on the offensive. However, success led to complacency and a need for money led to the introduction of payments in lieu of military service, and eventually the themes became incapable of providing sufficient troops, leading to the rising employment of mercenaries instead. Allies: The army may have allies from the Steppe Nomads (Alans, Khazars or Pechenegs) and Rus lists.

ARMY COMPOSITION:
[Thematic Byzantine]
  Cavalry: at least 25%
  Infantry and Skirmishers: up to 50%
  An army may be composed entirely of Thematic troops, or a combination of thematic or tagmatic troops. If any tagmata are used, a tagmatic general must be present. Characters: Up to 25% Infantry &amp; Skirmishers: Up to 50% (Up to 66% before 867 AD) Cavalry: At least 25% Special Units: Up to 25% Artillery: may also take Light Bolt Throwers (without cart) from the Late Roman list with one per 1,200 pts. Allies: The army may have allies from the Steppe Nomads (Alans, Khazars or Pechenegs) and Rus lists.</description>
    </rule>
  </sharedRules>
